📅 Day 27 – Transactions, Commit & Rollback in JDBC

Full Stack Web Development with Java Internship

Today’s session focused on database transaction management in JDBC, particularly the concepts of commit and rollback. The mentor explained how transactions help maintain data consistency and integrity when multiple database operations are performed together.

🔹 Topics Covered
1️⃣ Database Transactions

A transaction is a group of database operations executed as a single logical unit of work.

Key points discussed:

Ensures data consistency

All operations succeed or none are applied

Prevents partial updates in the database

2️⃣ Commit Operation

commit() permanently saves changes to the database.

Used when all SQL operations in a transaction execute successfully.

After commit, changes cannot be undone.

Example:

connection.commit();
3️⃣ Rollback Operation

rollback() reverses changes if an error occurs during transaction execution.

Ensures the database returns to its previous consistent state.

Example:

connection.rollback();
4️⃣ Auto-Commit Mode

By default, JDBC runs in auto-commit mode.

Each SQL statement is automatically committed.

To manage transactions manually:

connection.setAutoCommit(false);
🔹 Practical Implementation

During the practical session, I implemented programs that:

Executed multiple SQL statements within a transaction

Disabled auto-commit mode

Used commit() to save successful operations

Used rollback() to revert changes when an exception occurred

This helped me understand how transaction management maintains database integrity and reliability.